## 1. The Big Idea

_One sentence: **A good face routine is two short passes a day onto one surface — Cleanse → Treat → Moisturize → Protect — where the morning's sunscreen is the load-bearing slab that carries ~90% of the result, the "serum" is just a swappable slot you drop one ranked active into (retinoid first), and the fastest way to go backward in the tropics is to keep feeding actives and oils to a fungal problem they cannot fix.** The poster must make the reader feel, instantly: the proportion (SPF dwarfs everything), the swap (the treat step is a cartridge, not a product), the two asymmetric passes (AM defends, PM repairs), and the one trap that turns "doing more" into harm._

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure — the hero object

**A 24-HOUR COAT SCHEDULE: the skin surface drawn as a horizontal substrate datum, with the topical assembly applied as dimensioned coats in two passes across the day — an AM pass (defense) on the left under daylight, a PM pass (repair) on the right under dark — and the treat step rendered as a swappable ACTIVE CARTRIDGE rather than a fixed product.** This is the anchor; nothing replaces it. It is the working-drawing sequel to 1.1's wall section: the same `barrier`, `substrate`, `load-bearing step` language the article uses, now drawn as a build sequence on a clock.

Read **left → right (AM → PM), and bottom → top within each coat (application order, thinnest→thickest):**

- **THE SURFACE DATUM (runs along the bottom, full width).** A single horizontal line = the stratum corneum / barrier, the substrate everything docks onto (direct callback to 1.1's wall). A faint daylight-to-dark gradient washes left→right across the band so AM and PM read as two halves of one day without needing a clock face.

- **THE AM PASS (left half) — the defense shift.** Four coats applied left-to-right onto the surface, each a dimensioned layer with its thickness encoding leverage:
  - `CLEANSE` — thin prep coat. Caption: `gentle, pH ~5.5 · over-cleansing = barrier damage (the #1 self-inflicted problem).`
  - `THE ACTIVE ▸ slot` — the cartridge bay (see below), here loaded with `VITAMIN C` (AM antioxidant + collagen cofactor, pairs with SPF).
  - `MOISTURIZE` — humectant→emollient→occlusive; `lightweight gel-cream for the tropics.`
  - `PROTECT · SPF` — **the dominant slab, by far the tallest/thickest element in the whole poster**, capping the AM pass and intercepting a small UV arrow carried over from 1.1 (red, the only place red touches a *force*). Captions: `SPF 30–50 · broad-spectrum · PA++++` and the cardinal rule `REAPPLY every 2h outdoors — more with sweat.` This slab must visibly out-thickness the other three AM coats combined — the 90% made literal, exactly as 1.1's PROTECT layer.

- **THE PM PASS (right half) — the repair shift.** Three coats, no SPF, no UV arrow (the night is the repair window):
  - `CLEANSE` — `double-cleanse only if you wore SPF/heavy product (oil cleanser → gentle wash).`
  - `THE ACTIVE ▸ slot` — the cartridge bay, here loaded with `RETINOID` (the engine), with a small honest note `PM only · photolabile · start 2–3×/wk · expect "retinization" purge` and a hard pointer `topical tretinoin ≠ oral isotretinoin (accutane → Part 2.1).`
  - `MOISTURIZE` — `"buffer" for sensitive skin; GHK-Cu/peptides on non-retinoid nights.`
  The visible asymmetry — **AM ends in a giant SPF slab, PM ends in nothing** — is itself a teaching: defense by day, repair by night.

- **THE SWAPPABLE ACTIVE CARTRIDGE (the signature new device).** The treat step in both passes is not a product — it is a **bay** labeled `THE ACTIVE — "serum" is a format, not an ingredient. The question is which active.` Docked beside or beneath the schedule is a **ranked cartridge rack**: small interchangeable instrument cards, each `name · mechanism → use → (OTC dose) → best-for`, ordered by evidence with the retinoid first. Tier-1 cartridges are solid-outlined; Tier-2 (research / grey-market) cartridges are hatched with a QC/sourcing tick. The rack makes the central reframe physical: you slot *one* in, you don't buy the whole aisle.

- **THE WAIT-TIME CLEARANCES & THE NO-CO-APPLY INTERLOCK.** Between coats, draw **dimensioned gaps** (the same dimension-tick device as 1.1's layer thicknesses) for layering wait-times. A small **compatibility interlock** card encodes the rules as an instrument, not prose: `RETINOID + AHA/BHA → don't stack while ramping` · `L-ASCORBIC + NIACINAMIDE → separate if irritation-prone` · `VITAMIN C + SPF → AM synergy ✓` · global rule `thinnest → thickest.`

- **THE TONER DEMOTION (the quiet correction).** Off the critical spine, draw toner / K-beauty essence (the medicube category) as a **thin dashed, removable optional layer** with a leader line to a parked caption: `optional hydration — not load-bearing · never at the cost of SPF or a retinoid. "feels like it works" ≠ measured.` This is the article's epistemic-discipline move, drawn as a layer you can lift out without the building falling.

- **THE ONE RED OBJECT — THE FUNGAL-ACNE TRAP (the failure device, reused discipline).** Per the locked single-alarm-red rule, exactly one red failure mark: a boxed **wrong-tool warning** showing an active (and an oil) being applied onto a *fungal* condition that they can't fix — and feeding it. `FUNGAL ACNE (Malassezia) ≠ bacterial acne` · `benzoyl peroxide + most actives don't work` · `esters / fatty-acid oils FEED it` → fix: `antifungal — ketoconazole / zinc pyrithione / sulphur · lightweight gel · reapply SPF when you sweat.` This is the exact functional twin of 1.1's reversed-assembly ghost and Hair's breach interlock: a single ghosted-or-boxed counter-state that teaches by showing "doing more" make it worse. It also carries the article's tropical payload.

**The two-register surface (the signature move, reused).** Everything in the **correct schedule is the "build register"** — crisp blueprint linework, dimensioned coats and clearances, warm tissue. The **fungal-acne trap is the "fault register"** — red-tagged, the active mis-applied, the problem visibly fed. The surface teaches right-tool vs wrong-tool before a word is read.

Why this metaphor fits and nothing else does: the article's literal structure is *an ordered four-step sequence* (Cleanse→Treat→Moisturize→Protect), *a proportion* (90 SPF/consistency vs 10 active), *a swap* ("serum is a format, not an ingredient"), *a two-pass asymmetric day* (AM defends / PM repairs with wait-times and no-co-apply rules), and *one wrong-tool failure* (fungal acne). A 24-hour coat schedule with a swappable cartridge, dimensioned wait-time clearances, an SPF slab whose thickness is the leverage, and a single red wrong-tool box is the only object that holds all of them at once. A flat-lay of bottles would make the 10% the whole game; a circular routine-clock would hide the AM/PM asymmetry and the leverage proportion — both are the precise errors the article refuses.

## 3. Layout Description

**Landscape / wide (~16:9 or 3:2) is primary** — the hero is a day read left→right, so the long axis is time. (Deliberate flip from Skin 1.1's portrait section: 1.1 froze the wall; 2.0 runs the schedule.) A tall-square variant works for inline blog use by stacking AM over PM, but the AM→PM left→right read is preferred.

- **Top band (~12%):** the headline (see §4) set small on the bone paper, kerned wide, thin drafting border beneath, and a slug `Part 2.0 of 4 · Skin · the Attractive pillar`. A faint `AM → PM` arrow and a `FACE ONLY · TOPICALS ONLY` scope tag run under the border (honoring the article's hard scope warning so the poster can't be misread as the whole skin system).
- **Center stage (~58%) — the hero schedule:** the surface datum runs full width with the daylight→dark wash; the AM pass (four coats, ending in the dominant SPF slab + small red UV arrow) fills the left, the PM pass (three coats, ending in moisturizer, no SPF) fills the right; dimensioned wait-time clearances sit between coats; the swappable cartridge bays are highlighted at each treat step. The SPF slab is the highest-information object and must survive a thumbnail — if you only see one thing, you see the giant morning sunscreen.
- **Lower-left dock — the cartridge rack (~16%):** the ranked active catalog (retinoid #1 → vitamin C → niacinamide → AHA/BHA → azelaic [flag III–V/PIH] → peptide/GHK-Cu), Tier-1 solid vs Tier-2 hatched, each with mechanism/dose/best-for. This is where the "which active" decision lives.
- **Lower-right dock — the spec sheet (~16%, cool slate register):** three small instrument cards —
  - **Compatibility interlock:** the no-co-apply rules + thinnest→thickest.
  - **Tropical & sourcing card:** `humidity → lighter, less occlusive · sweat → reapply SPF` · `Malaysia: adapalene (Differin) OTC · tretinoin via clinic Rx · azelaic/niacinamide/vit-C pharmacy + Shopee/Watsons/Guardian · pick III–V-friendly Asian/Korean/Japanese SPF (no white cast).`
  - **The 90/10 frame bar:** a single proportion bar `consistency + SPF ≈ 90%` over a thin `which active ≈ 10%` — the visual echo of the SPF slab above.
- **The fungal-acne trap** sits as a bordered red box bridging the docks or just beneath the PM pass (it applies to the whole tropical routine), clearly the one alarm in the frame.
- **Bottom band (~10%):** the thesis lockup (see §4) + the four-step micro-strip `CLEANSE → TREAT → MOISTURIZE → PROTECT`, the four chips sized to match the coat thicknesses above so strip and hero agree.

Reading path: **enter at the headline → drop onto the AM pass and feel the giant SPF slab dominate → notice the treat step is a slot, not a product → cross into the PM pass and feel it end in repair, not protection → glance to the cartridge rack to choose the one active → read the spec docks for the rules and the Malaysia sourcing → snag on the single red fungal-acne trap → exit on the four-step strip + 90/10 bar.**

