## 7. What to Avoid

- **A flat-lay / shelf / pyramid of product bottles as the hero.** A bottle line-up makes the **10% actives look like the whole game** — the exact "$90 serum, skip the sunscreen" error the article exists to refuse. Products appear only as small dimensioned labels on coats or as cartridge cards, never as the central object, and never bigger than the SPF slab.
- **A real glowing / before-after / dewy face, or a hand applying cream.** Says "skincare ad," teaches nothing, and hides the order, the proportion, and the AM/PM asymmetry. Never make a real face the hero. (A small Fitzpatrick III–V swatch may sit *inside* a spec card if needed, never as the anchor.)
- **A circular "routine clock" or a single undifferentiated 10-step list.** A clock hides the AM-defends / PM-repairs asymmetry and flattens the SPF leverage; a 10-step list reinstates the bottle-aisle mindset. Keep it a two-pass schedule with one dominant slab. (Hair 2.0 already avoided the circular routine-clock — hold the line.)
- **Drawing the four steps at equal size, or drawing the active as a fixed branded product.** Equal sizes collapse the SPF-is-90% point; a fixed product kills the "serum is a slot, not an ingredient" reframe. The SPF slab must visibly dominate, and the treat step must read as a *swappable bay*.
- **Letting Part 2.1 / 3.0 content leak in.** No accutane, no injectable GHK-Cu/GH, no lasers/peels/microneedling, no body/neck/hands. Those are other articles; this poster is *face, topical, daily* — show only `→ 2.1` / `→ 3.0` pointers if a bridge is unavoidable.
